## Question

Does a restrained persistent Operations Center improve anticipation, progress understanding, tool desirability, and return behavior enough to justify the navigation, visual, and implementation cost?

## Variant A: direct mission

- learner enters First Contact immediately;
- current objective, code, result, coach, and progress are inside the mission;
- completion uses a compact capability and next-action panel.

## Variant B: hub plus mission

- learner sees a restrained Operations Center at entry or natural boundaries;
- one active case, one available tool, one locked possibility, and one recommended mission are visible;
- the hub visibly changes after the mission.

## Control variables

Keep the following equivalent:

- First Contact content;
- visual quality;
- code task;
- feedback;
- reward evidence;
- accessibility; and
- overall session length.

## Prototype requirements

- [ ] no installation required
- [ ] high-quality responsive visual treatment
- [ ] scripted code-to-world reaction is acceptable
- [ ] keyboard path
- [ ] reduced-motion path
- [ ] clean Continue and Stop
- [ ] reload or revisit state
- [ ] observer notes and comparison capture

## Measure

- time to first meaningful code action;
- navigation hesitation;
- case comprehension;
- interest in future tools;
- understanding of progress;
- voluntary exploration;
- desire to continue or return;
- remembered learning versus remembered decoration;
- perceived quality and cohesion; and
- additional authoring and engineering cost.

## Resolution output

Record one of:

- mission-only;
- hub-plus-mission;
- hybrid hub only at natural boundaries; or
- remove the hub concept.

